  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 121 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSunday and Monday...Upper system exits the state Sunday morning, \nwith just some lingering isolated showers across the southeast \ncorner through the morning hours. Temporary ridging then fills in \nacross the region through Monday, with the ridge axis pushing east \nby Monday afternoon as the next feature approaches from the west. \nPlan on dry conditions with initially cool temps, moderating on \nMonday. Highs on Sunday will be in the 40s to around 50F, then \nclimbing into the 50s for the plains on Monday, while the high \nvalleys warm into the 40s to around 50F.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Long range models indicate that another \nquick-moving system across the area mid-week, with the EC showing a \ndeeper southern track while the operational GFS indicates a more \nglancing blow. Regardless, the trend is that this feature will bring \nmuch stronger southwest winds along with a similar amount of QPF \ncompared to the Friday/Saturday system. This will amount to a period \nof wind-driven snow, focuses across the southwest mts and which will \nlikely prompt another highlight at some point. Another difference \nwith this system is that it looks like effects will be tied to the \nhigher terrain, while the plains will remain dry. High temps across \nthe plains both days will be in the upper 50s to mid 60s, while the \nhigh valleys will warm into the upper 40s to mid 50s on Tue, then \njust the 40s on Wed.\n\nThursday and beyond...Models paint a third system to affect the \nregion starting Thursday evening, with some indications that this \ncould be a stronger feature. Forecast snow amounts along the \nContinental Divide, though far too early, lean towards a significant \nevent. Stay tuned. Moore",
